lesson.body:
An **applicant** (registered, or wanting to register) can seek a binding ruling from the **Authority for Advance Ruling (AAR)** of the State, which has one member from CGST and one from SGST.

**Questions allowed (section 97(2)):**

- (a) classification of goods or services;
- (b) applicability of a notification;
- (c) determination of time and value of supply;
- (d) admissibility of ITC;
- (e) determination of liability to pay tax;
- (f) whether registration is required;
- (g) whether an activity amounts to supply.

**Place of supply is not an allowed question**, because State authorities cannot decide inter-State matters.

**Procedure:** application in ARA-01 with fee (₹5,000 each under CGST and SGST). The AAR must not admit a question already pending or decided in any proceedings of the applicant. Ruling within **90 days** of the application. If the two members differ, the question goes to the Appellate Authority for Advance Ruling (AAAR).

**Appeal:** the applicant or the jurisdictional officer can appeal to the **AAAR** within **30 days** (extendable by 30 days); the AAAR decides within 90 days. No further appeal lies under the Act, but writ jurisdiction remains. Section 101A provides for a National Appellate Authority to resolve conflicting rulings of different States; check its notified status before relying on it.

**Binding effect (section 103):** only on the applicant and the jurisdictional officer, and only while the law and facts remain the same. A ruling obtained by **fraud, suppression or misrepresentation** is void ab initio (section 104).
